Host test for CONFIG_CMDLINE_SPOOF_LOCK_STATE
=============================================

    make -C tools/testing/cmdline-spoof check

lib/cmdline_spoof.c and fs/proc/cmdline.c are compiled straight into
test_cmdline.c against the small kernel stubs under include/, so the code
exercised here is the code that ships rather than a reimplementation of it.
The real include/linux/cmdline_spoof.h is used, picked up from the source tree
after the stub directory on the include path, so a change to that interface
breaks this build instead of going unnoticed.

The Makefile builds two binaries from the one source, with and without
CONFIG_CMDLINE_SPOOF_LOCK_STATE, and both run the same table of cases. Each
case pins the exact byte sequence cmdline_proc_show() emits, trailing newline
included. test-disabled expects every command line back verbatim, which is what
proves the option is inert when it is off.

The table covers token position, repeated occurrences, whitespace runs and
tabs, the green/yellow/red values that must pass through, near-miss spellings
that must not be rewritten, all three lock-state keys, and quoting. The quoting
cases matter most: argument boundaries follow next_arg() in lib/cmdline.c, so

    extra="before androidboot.verifiedbootstate=orange after"

is one argument and must come back untouched. An earlier revision split on
whitespace alone and corrupted it.

Beyond the table there are checks that cmdline_spoof_len() agrees with what the
render actually produces, since that length is the size sysfs advertises for
chosen/bootargs; that cmdline_spoof_copy() truncates and terminates correctly
at every buffer size down to zero; and that reading a rendered property in
small chunks reassembles it exactly, NUL included, which is the contract
of_bootargs_read() depends on.

test_of_bootargs.c covers the device-tree half the same way, compiling the real
drivers/of/kobj.c so the shipped callbacks actually run: of_bootargs_property(),
of_bootargs_read(), of_node_property_read() and the size that
__of_add_property_sysfs() advertises. Registration itself is inert, since what
matters is the read contract. It checks the advertised size against what the
read delivers, whole and chunked reads, reads at an offset, at the end, past the
end, with a zero count and with a negative offset, that malformed properties
(unterminated, embedded NUL, zero length) fall back to raw bytes untouched, that
only chosen/bootargs is a target, and that a failed kmalloc returns -ENOMEM
rather than falling back to serving the real value. It needs -DCONFIG_OF_KOBJ,
because otherwise of_private.h supplies stub definitions that collide with the
real ones.

Both binaries also cover the reader exemption. Boot-critical readers must keep
seeing the real boot state, so cmdline_spoof_exempt_reader() returns
is_global_init(current). include/linux/sched.h stubs that behind
test_reader_is_global_init, which the tests flip to model a read by pid 1: every
case must then come back byte for byte, whole and chunked, while an ordinary
reader still gets the rewrite.

The stubs deliberately cover nothing beyond what the source files include. If
any of them grows a dependency, the build breaks here first, which is the
intended signal to extend include/ rather than to work around it. Allocation
failure is reachable through test_alloc_fail_countdown in include/linux/slab.h.

Nothing in the kernel build descends into this directory, so it has no effect
on building the kernel itself. For a sanitizer run:

    clang -O1 -g -fsanitize=address,undefined -I../../.. -Iinclude \
        -I../../../include -DCONFIG_CMDLINE_SPOOF_LOCK_STATE \
        -o /tmp/san test_cmdline.c
